<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="Workspace file" id="Workspace-file">

    <web-summary>
        Write a %tool_name% workspace file in YAML, JSON or TOML: declare your projects, sub-workspaces and remotes,
        with every key and validation error explained.
    </web-summary>

    <p>
        A workspace file describes what a folder contains: the projects cloned in it, the sub-workspaces it
        nests, and optionally the git remote of the workspace itself. %tool_name% reads this file to know which
        repositories to check, fetch, update or clone.
    </p>

    <chapter title="File name and location" id="workspace-file_chapter-location">
        <p>
            The file is named <code>workspaces</code> and can be written in YAML, JSON or TOML.
        </p>
        <table>
            <tr>
                <td>Extension</td>
                <td>Format</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>.yaml</code>, <code>.yml</code></td>
                <td>YAML</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>.json</code></td>
                <td>JSON</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>.toml</code></td>
                <td>TOML</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
        <p>%tool_name% looks for the file in this order and uses the first one found:</p>
        <list type="decimal">
            <li><path>.gws/workspaces.yaml</path>, <path>.gws/workspaces.yml</path>,
                <path>.gws/workspaces.json</path>, <path>.gws/workspaces.toml</path>
            </li>
            <li><path>workspaces.yaml</path>, <path>workspaces.yml</path>,
                <path>workspaces.json</path>, <path>workspaces.toml</path> at the root of the workspace
            </li>
        </list>
        <note>
            Keep a single file per workspace. If several are found, only the first one is used and
            %tool_name% prints a warning: <code>Several workspaces files found, please keep only one</code>.
        </note>
        <p>
            When %tool_name% creates the file itself, it uses <path>.gws/workspaces.yaml</path>. When it rewrites
            an existing file, the original format is kept.
        </p>
        <note>
            A rewritten file is generated again from its content: comments are lost, <code>url</code> becomes
            <code>remotes</code> and <code>folder</code> becomes <code>path</code>. Other keys, including
            <code>settings</code>, are kept.
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Structure" id="workspace-file_chapter-structure">
        <p>The file has five top-level keys.</p>
        <table>
            <tr>
                <td>Key</td>
                <td>Required</td>
                <td>Description</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>version</code></td>
                <td>Yes</td>
                <td>Version of the file format. Must be <code>1</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>self</code></td>
                <td>No</td>
                <td>Git remotes of the workspace folder itself.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>workspaces</code></td>
                <td>No</td>
                <td>List of sub-workspaces.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>projects</code></td>
                <td>No</td>
                <td>List of repositories cloned in this workspace.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>settings</code></td>
                <td>No</td>
                <td>Free-form map. It is preserved when the file is rewritten but is not used yet.</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
        <warning>
            Unknown keys are rejected. A typo such as <code>projets</code> makes the file unreadable: it is
            ignored entirely, see <a href="#workspace-file_chapter-errors">Validation errors</a>.
        </warning>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Entries" id="workspace-file_chapter-entries">
        <p>
            Items of <code>workspaces</code> and <code>projects</code> share the same keys.
        </p>
        <table>
            <tr>
                <td>Key</td>
                <td>Description</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>path</code></td>
                <td>
                    Folder of the entry, relative to the workspace folder. Absolute paths and paths starting
                    with <code>~/</code> are also accepted.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>url</code></td>
                <td>Shorthand for a single remote named <code>origin</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>remotes</code></td>
                <td>
                    List of remotes. Each remote has a required <code>url</code> and an optional
                    <code>name</code>.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>folder</code></td>
                <td>
                    Workspaces only. Declares a plain folder with no git remote, used to group projects.
                    A workspace with a <code>path</code> and no remote is also a plain folder.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <chapter title="Rules" id="workspace-file_chapter-rules">
            <list>
                <li><code>url</code> and <code>remotes</code> cannot be used together.</li>
                <li>
                    A remote without a name is called <code>origin</code> when it is first in the list and
                    <code>upstream</code> when it is second. Name every remote beyond the second explicitly,
                    otherwise it is also called <code>origin</code>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A project requires <code>path</code> and either <code>url</code> or <code>remotes</code>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A workspace requires <code>path</code> or <code>folder</code>. A workspace with a remote is
                    a git workspace; <code>folder</code> cannot be combined with <code>path</code>,
                    <code>url</code> or <code>remotes</code>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A workspace whose <code>path</code> is <code>.</code> describes the workspace folder itself,
                    like <code>self</code>.
                </li>
            </list>
            <note>
                The file does not check that paths exist on disk.
            </note>
        </chapter>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Examples" id="workspace-file_chapter-examples">
        <p>The same workspace written in the three supported formats.</p>
        <tabs>
            <tab title="YAML">
                <code-block lang="yaml"><![CDATA[
version: 1
self:
  remotes:
    - { name: origin, url: git@example.com:org/root.git }
workspaces:
  - path: sub
  - path: libs
    remotes:
      - { url: git@example.com:org/libs.git }
projects:
  - path: api
    remotes:
      - { name: origin, url: git@example.com:org/api.git }
      - { url: git@example.com:up/api.git }
settings:
  custom: value
]]></code-block>
            </tab>
            <tab title="JSON">
                <code-block lang="json"><![CDATA[
{
  "version": 1,
  "self": {"remotes": [{"name": "origin", "url": "git@example.com:org/root.git"}]},
  "workspaces": [
    {"path": "sub"},
    {"path": "libs", "remotes": [{"url": "git@example.com:org/libs.git"}]}
  ],
  "projects": [
    {"path": "api", "remotes": [{"name": "origin", "url": "git@example.com:org/api.git"}, {"url": "git@example.com:up/api.git"}]}
  ],
  "settings": {"custom": "value"}
}
]]></code-block>
            </tab>
            <tab title="TOML">
                <code-block lang="toml"><![CDATA[
version = 1

[self]
remotes = [{ name = "origin", url = "git@example.com:org/root.git" }]

[[workspaces]]
path = "sub"

[[workspaces]]
path = "libs"
remotes = [{ url = "git@example.com:org/libs.git" }]

[[projects]]
path = "api"
remotes = [{ name = "origin", url = "git@example.com:org/api.git" }, { url = "git@example.com:up/api.git" }]

[settings]
custom = "value"
]]></code-block>
            </tab>
        </tabs>

        <p>
            In this example the second remote of <code>api</code> has no name, so it is named
            <code>upstream</code>.
        </p>

        <p>With the <code>url</code> and <code>folder</code> shorthands:</p>
        <code-block lang="yaml"><![CDATA[
version: 1
workspaces:
  - folder: ./existing
  - path: child
    url: git@example.com:org/child.git
projects:
  - path: api
    url: git@example.com:org/api.git
]]></code-block>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Validation errors" id="workspace-file_chapter-errors">
        <p>
            When the file is invalid, %tool_name% prints a <code>Failed to read workspace configuration</code>
            warning with one of the following messages and ignores the whole file. Commands then stop with
            <code>No projects or workspaces found</code>. Messages about an entry are prefixed with its
            position, such as <code>projects[0]</code> or <code>workspaces[1] libs</code>.
        </p>
        <table>
            <tr>
                <td>Message</td>
                <td>Fix</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>unsupported &lt;file&gt; version N (supported: 1)</code></td>
                <td>Set <code>version: 1</code>. A missing version is also refused.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>failed to parse &lt;file&gt;: ...</code></td>
                <td>Fix the syntax, or remove the unknown key named in the message.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>path or folder is required</code></td>
                <td>Give the workspace a <code>path</code> or a <code>folder</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>folder cannot be combined with path, url or remotes</code></td>
                <td>Keep only <code>folder</code>, or switch to <code>path</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>folder is not allowed on a project</code></td>
                <td>Use <code>path</code> in <code>projects</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>path is required</code></td>
                <td>Give the project a <code>path</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>url cannot be combined with remotes</code></td>
                <td>Use either <code>url</code> or <code>remotes</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>url or remotes is required</code></td>
                <td>Tell %tool_name% where to clone the project from.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>remotes[N]: empty url</code></td>
                <td>Fill the <code>url</code> of the remote.</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <chapter title="Duplicates" id="workspace-file_chapter-duplicates">
            <p>
                Two <code>workspaces</code> entries, or two <code>projects</code> entries, with the same
                <code>path</code> are not a file error but make every command stop with
                <code>Workspace is in invalid state, please run '%tool_call% doctor' to show diagnostics</code>.
                Run <code>%tool_call% doctor run</code> to see the failed check, and
                <code>%tool_call% doctor run --autofix</code> to keep the first entry and remove the others.
            </p>
        </chapter>

        <chapter title="Nested workspaces" id="workspace-file_chapter-nested">
            <p>
                Nested workspaces are loaded up to a depth of 10. Deeper workspaces are reported with
                <code>maximum workspace depth (10) reached</code>, and a workspace reached twice with
                <code>workspace already visited (possible cycle)</code>. Both are shown as an error on that
                workspace and the rest of the tree is still loaded. A sub-workspace whose folder does not exist
                is listed but not read.
            </p>
        </chapter>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="See also" id="workspace-file_chapter-see-also">
        <list>
            <li><a href="Hook.topic">Hook</a></li>
            <li><a href="Completion.topic">Completion</a></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>
</topic>
